  "title": "The Manifest Unfolds",
  "text": "Rain hammers the window panes as you pull the thin, coffee‑stained sheet from the log cabinet. The handwriting is crisp, the ink barely smudged by humidity. Across the top, the word Kestrel is stamped in bold, and beneath it two names: Mara Linde and Jorge Ruiz. Their roles are listed—engineer and communications tech—along with a scheduled return time of 23:30, already past. You note the absence of any signature confirming they boarded the rescue boat; the paper is silent on their fate. The manifest confirms the vessel drifting beyond the breakwater, powerless, and that the two technicians are still listed as aboard, not yet rescued. You trace the ink with a fingertip, feeling the paper’s slight waviness, and file the detail into the log, the rain’s rhythm matching the ticking of the station clock as it edges toward midnight."
}

{
  "title": "Charting the Unseen",
  "text": "Rain hammers the window panes as the old clock ticks toward midnight. The recorder crackles, your own weary voice pleading not to pull the switch, and three low notes linger in the stale air. You glance at the laminated coastal chart pinned to the wall, its ink faded but still legible. The marked Kestrel, a maintenance boat, sits just beyond the breakwater, its silhouette a dark smear against the churning surf. Beside it, the grid overlay reads \"Grid C-7\" in bold black. You pull a fresh sheet from the log, note the discrepancy between the recording's timestamp and the station clock, then copy the coordinates: \"Grid C-7, beyond the breakwater.\" The entry is clear, no rescue request yet sent, the chart confirming the boat's helpless drift. The paper rustles softly as you set the pen down, the rain's rhythm the only other sound, and the shutdown switch remains warm under your fingertip, waiting for the next decision before the clock strikes twelve."
}

{
  "title": "Diagram of a Choice",
  "text": "You pull the maintenance diagram from the drawer, its edges curled from years of handling. The lamp on your desk casts a thin pool of light over the faded schematics, and the rain drums a steady rhythm against the window. Tracing the lines with your fingertip, you see the main transmission frequency loop back into the damaged relay, a feedback path that could amplify any signal you send. Beside the relay symbol, a handwritten note in the margin reads: \"Use channel nine\"—a bypass instruction scrawled in ink that has not faded. The notation is clear: reroute the output through channel nine to avoid the faulty relay entirely. You note the instruction in your log, the ink darkening under the lamp, but you keep your hand steady on the shutdown switch. No transmission is made; you simply record the safe route, the rain still hissing outside, the clock ticking toward midnight."}

{
  "title": "A Timestamp Out of Sync",
  "text": "You stare at the glowing station clock, its hands trembling toward midnight. The recorder’s digital readout blinks 00:00, a full twenty minutes ahead of the 23:40 you see on the wall. Your pen hovers over the log sheet, the paper cold under your fingertips. You write, in neat block letters, \"Recording timestamp 00:00 – twenty minutes ahead of station clock 23:40. No transmission sent yet.\" The rain hammers the window, a steady percussion that masks the low notes still echoing from the receiver. The breath that follows the notes seems to linger, but you keep your focus on the paper, noting the discrepancy without adding interpretation. The shutdown switch remains untouched, the transmitter idle. The station hums around you, the only certainty the ticking of the clock and the wet scent of sea air seeping through the panes."
